{"name":"Regent St Blue","hex":"#a0cdd9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"regent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who rules in place of the monarch, especially because the monarch is too young, absent, or disabled. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any ruler."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Ruling; governing; regnant.","Exercising vicarious authority."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/regent"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Regent Street","blocks":[{"text":"Regent Street is a major shopping street in the West End of London. It is named after George, the Prince Regent and was laid out under the direction of the architect John Nash and the owner James Burton. It runs from Waterloo Place in St James's at the southern end, through Piccadilly Circus and Oxford Circus, to All Souls Church. From there Langham Place and Portland Place continue the route to Regent's Park."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Regent_Street_2011-04-25.jpg/330px-Regent_Street_2011-04-25.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regent_Street","wikidata":"Q1123106"},{"title":"Regent","blocks":[{"text":"In a monarchy, a regent is a person appointed to execute the office of a monarch temporarily, essentially as a substitute ruler. Regencies may arise for a number of reasons, including the monarch being a minor, ill, absent from the country, or otherwise unavailable. A regent may also be appointed in cases where the throne is vacant, or the identity of the legitimate monarch is disputed. The rule of a regent or regents is called a regency."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Sture_Sten_Svantesson_d.y._1492-1520%2C_och_Kristina_Gyllenstierna%2C_1494-1559_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15066.tif/lossless-page1-330px-Sture_Sten_Svantesson_d.y._1492-1520%2C_och_Kristina_Gyllenstierna%2C_1494-1559_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15066.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regent","wikidata":"Q477406"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg/500px-Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg/1280px-Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg/500px-Oxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Panoramic view of Oxford Circus; the location where Oxford Street meets Regent Street","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOxford_Circus_Panorama_March_2006.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Regent_St_proposal_published_1813.jpg/500px-Regent_St_proposal_published_1813.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Regent_St_proposal_published_1813.jpg/500px-Regent_St_proposal_published_1813.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Regent Street proposal, published 1813, titled \"PLAN, presented to the House of Commons, of a STREET proposed from CHARING CROSS to PORTLAND PLACE, leading to the Crown Estate in Marylebone Park\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARegent_St_proposal_published_1813.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Quadrant%2C_Regent_Street_engraved_by_J.Woods_after_J.Salmon_publ_1837_edited.jpg/500px-Quadrant%2C_Regent_Street_engraved_by_J.Woods_after_J.Salmon_publ_1837_edited.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Quadrant%2C_Regent_Street_engraved_by_J.Woods_after_J.Salmon_publ_1837_edited.jpg/500px-Quadrant%2C_Regent_Street_engraved_by_J.Woods_after_J.Salmon_publ_1837_edited.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Quadrant, Regent Street in 1837, seen from Piccadilly Circus. The buildings have since been replaced.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuadrant%2C_Regent_Street_engraved_by_J.Woods_after_J.Salmon_publ_1837_edited.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w